**Reused Evidence**
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["6B.1"]}
- Reused only as ruler-period attribution context: it anchors Kishida’s 2022 office and authority, not welfare performance by itself.

**New Evidence**
- Kishida framed 2022 policy around COVID response, wage increases, doubling public/private investment in people, skills/re-education, and an all-generations social security system.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy Speech by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio to the 208th Session of the Diet","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-01-17","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202201/_00009.html","claim":"Kishida made wage growth, larger public and private investment in people, skills and re-education, and a sustainable social security system oriented to all generations central to his 2022 policy agenda.","locator":"lines 164-187","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, dated prime-ministerial policy speech from the target period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (January 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida statement as prime minister; strong attribution to ruler intent and policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["This is an announced agenda, not proof of full implementation in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Kishida tied women’s financial independence to his new-capitalism agenda and said he would address wage gaps and choice in work.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Video Message by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio on the Occasion of International Women’s Day","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-03-08","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202203/_00011.html","claim":"Kishida said financial independence was the key to addressing women’s structural disadvantages and linked his cabinet’s agenda to correcting wage gaps and letting women work as they choose.","locator":"lines 20-25","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, dated prime-ministerial statement with direct policy language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (March 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida statement; strong attribution to ruler intent on gender and opportunity policy.","contrary_evidence":["The message is rhetorical unless paired with later implementation evidence."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The labor ministry made gender pay-gap disclosure mandatory for large firms, turning a Kishida-linked new-capitalism proposal into a concrete transparency rule.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"MHLW revised the Ordinance and Notification under the Women’s Active Engagement Promotion Act","publisher":"Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, Japan","publication_date":"2022-07-08","url":"https://www.mhlw.go.jp/stf/newpage_26587.html","claim":"The ministry added gender pay-gap disclosure to required reporting and made it mandatory for companies with 301 or more employees.","locator":"lines 275-287","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry rule-change notice with a specific effective date and enforceable obligation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (July 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-era regulatory action tied in the notice to the new-capitalism execution plan; attribution is to Kishida administration rather than personal drafting.","contrary_evidence":["The page shows rule design, not whether firms improved equality immediately."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.5","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- October 2022 labor rules expanded parental leave flexibility and allowed both parents to split leave into two periods.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"育児休業 (Childcare leave)","publisher":"Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, Japan","publication_date":"2022-10-01","url":"https://www.mhlw.go.jp/seisakunitsuite/bunya/koyou_roudou/koyoukintou/ryouritsu/ikuji/childcare/","claim":"From October 1, 2022, both parents could divide childcare leave into two separate periods, expanding practical access to leave and care work sharing.","locator":"lines 80-92","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry rule summary with the 2022 effective date stated plainly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (effective October 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-era labor policy implementation; attribution is to Kishida government policy environment, not a personal administrative act.","contrary_evidence":["The page is a standing制度 page, so it does not show uptake or distributional effects."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The July 2022 price-response package included fertilizer-cost compensation to support food production and household welfare.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-07-08","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202207/_00008.html","claim":"Kishida announced a new assistance system compensating 70 percent of fertilizer cost increases and said the money would be delivered quickly using reserve funds.","locator":"lines 74-75","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, dated prime-ministerial announcement with a precise policy amount and timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (July 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida announcement as prime minister; strong attribution to crisis-response policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["This is a sectoral support measure, not proof of broad household welfare gains."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}

- The June 2022 child welfare reform strengthened family support and created the basis for child-family centers and more integrated local support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"令和4年６月に成立した改正児童福祉法について","publisher":"Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, Japan","publication_date":"2022-06","url":"https://www.mhlw.go.jp/stf/seisakunitsuite/bunya/kodomo/kodomo_kosodate/jidouhukushihou_kaisei.html","claim":"The 2022 revised Child Welfare Act package strengthened child and family support, including municipal child-family center development and more integrated local support for children and families in difficulty.","locator":"page summary section, lines 266-286 and linked June 2022 reform materials","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry reform page; publication month is clear, but the page itself is a hub and not a single law text excerpt.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (June 2022 reform)","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-era social-policy reform under Kishida; attribution is to the administration and ministry system rather than sole personal authorship.","contrary_evidence":["The hub page does not itself measure implementation or outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- In September 2022 the ministry said it was launching a new comprehensive child-abuse prevention package because consultations and fatal abuse cases kept rising.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"児童虐待防止対策の更なる推進について","publisher":"Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, Japan","publication_date":"2022-09-05","url":"https://www.mhlw.go.jp/stf/newpage_27671.html","claim":"The government said child-abuse consultation cases had continued to rise and abuse-related deaths had not ceased, and it adopted a new comprehensive countermeasure package including nationwide rollout of child-family centers and stronger child-consultation capacity.","locator":"lines 283-290","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry press release with a clear date and explicit policy response to worsening harm.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (September 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet and ministry action under Kishida; strong attribution to policy response, though the underlying abuse problem predates him.","contrary_evidence":["The page acknowledges worsening abuse indicators, so it is partly a response to failure rather than a clean success story."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Kishida’s October 2022 policy speech paired inclusion, child-rearing support, women’s participation, loneliness measures, and a new child-safety response after the nursery-school shuttle death.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy Speech by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio to the 210th Session of the Diet","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-10-03","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202210/_00003.html","claim":"Kishida said a diverse society, a social security system for all generations, stronger child-rearing support, active participation by women, loneliness and isolation measures, and urgent nursery-bus safety steps were part of his 2022 agenda.","locator":"lines 172-180","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, dated prime-ministerial speech with direct policy commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (October 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida policy speech; strong attribution to ruler intent and agenda-setting.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is agenda-setting and does not alone show downstream results."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- In December 2022 Kishida announced broad cost-of-living support, universal child-related assistance, and the coming launch of the Children and Families Agency.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-28","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00004.html","claim":"Kishida said the government would provide average households 47,000 yen in energy and LNG assistance, continue 100,000 yen in child-raising support without income caps, raise the childbirth lump-sum allowance to 500,000 yen, and move ahead with the Children and Families Agency.","locator":"lines 42-53","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, dated prime-ministerial press conference with specific amounts and institutional steps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target period (December 2022)","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida announcement as prime minister; strong attribution to the administration’s crisis and family policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["The relief is broad but temporary, and the page does not show whether it reversed 2022 real-income losses."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The statistics bureau showed that worker-household nominal income rose in 2022 but real income fell, indicating a real purchasing-power squeeze.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Yearly Average Survey Results, Family Income and Expenditure Survey","publisher":"Statistics Bureau, Japan","publication_date":"2023-02-28","url":"https://www.stat.go.jp/english/info/news/20230228.html","claim":"For worker households in 2022, monthly income rose 2.0 percent nominally but fell 1.0 percent in real terms, while real consumption also lagged inflation.","locator":"news release summary bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national statistics release, directly measuring household purchasing power for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year outcome released immediately after the window","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context only; not uniquely attributable to Kishida, but it conditions the 2022 welfare assessment.","contrary_evidence":["Nominal income rose even as real income fell, so the picture is mixed rather than uniformly negative."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- UNICEF’s Japan child-SDG assessment shows near-universal water, sanitation, and vaccination coverage, plus broad child social protection coverage.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Japan","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/jpn/","claim":"Japan’s child-related indicators show 98 percent measles vaccination, 99 percent DTP3 vaccination, 99 to 100 percent basic water and sanitation coverage, and 85 percent child social-protection coverage (latest listed as 2018 for that indicator).","locator":"country dashboard indicators, especially lines 4943 and 5049-5064","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF data dashboard with clearly reported indicator values, though the page is a data hub and not a Kishida policy document.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Country-level baseline context overlapping the target period","ruler_attribution":"Baseline context only; these are inherited system indicators rather than evidence of Kishida-specific delivery in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The child-social-protection figure is dated 2018, so it is not a 2022 ruler outcome."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- OECD’s workplace report shows Japan was already tackling skills access, career guidance, training participation, and teleworking adoption in response to a changing labor market.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The New Workplace in Japan: Skills for a Strong Recovery","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/the-new-workplace-in-japan_7c897f52-en.html","claim":"The OECD said Japan’s pandemic-era adult-learning response focused on improving career guidance, broadening training participation, and fostering teleworking, while also addressing inequalities in skills and training across socio-demographic groups.","locator":"abstract, lines 3733-3735","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary OECD country report, closely timed to the target year and specific about policy efforts and constraints.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year policy context","ruler_attribution":"Mostly system-level context; it overlaps with Kishida’s human-investment agenda but is not itself a Kishida decision record.","contrary_evidence":["The report is analytical context, not proof of Kishida’s direct authorship or 2022 implementation success."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- OECD’s public-health review reinforces Japan’s high-health baseline and the demographic pressures that Kishida inherited.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Reviews of Public Health: Japan","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2017-03-02","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-reviews-of-public-health-japan_9789264311602-en.html","claim":"The OECD described Japan as having the longest life expectancy in the OECD and very strong prevention systems, while noting the challenge of rapid population ageing and chronic disease.","locator":"executive summary lines 3737-3738","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary OECD review with a precise country assessment and clear structural constraints.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline and constraint context for 2022","ruler_attribution":"Context only; it supports the inherited baseline rather than Kishida-specific causation.","contrary_evidence":["A strong inherited public-health system limits how much credit can be assigned to a single 2022 administration."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across PMO, MHLW, MEXT, Statistics Bureau, OECD, UNICEF, World Bank, WHO, and Reuters/republished Reuters.
- Kept the strongest non-duplicative set that gave both policy intent and at least some outcome/constraint evidence.
- Rejected as duplicates or near-duplicates: repeated Kishida speeches on new capitalism, children, women, and social security across late 2021, 2022, and 2023; the 2022 PMO speeches were enough.
- Rejected as weak or context-only when they did not add material 2022 attribution: generic pension, care, housing, and welfare landing pages; broad WHO donor pages; World Bank country overview pages; older child-benefit history pages; and high-level OECD/UNICEF global reports.
- Rejected as out-of-period or too future-facing: 2023-2025 implementation pages for child-policy and scholarship expansions, 2024-2025 labor and education updates, and later ceremony pages that were only retrospective.
- Rejected some sources because they were better used as background than chapter evidence: the more general social-security and care-system pages, which describe durable institutions but do not pin 2022 choices to Kishida as clearly as the selected sources do.
- No major access block affected the final set; I did not need to rely on blocked or paywalled text.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct 2022 poverty-rate and child-poverty-rate data were not easily extractable in a clean English-language source set, so distributional assessment leans on real-income and purchasing-power evidence instead.
- There is still limited direct evidence on whether the 2022 child, family, and inflation measures measurably improved outcomes within 2022 rather than later.
- Subgroup-specific evidence is thin for disabled people, migrants, and regional disparities, so 6B.3 and 6B.8 remain partly inferential.
- More search would help on budget execution, service uptake, childcare availability, and whether Kishida’s new-capitalism measures changed actual household life chances in 2022 rather than just setting the policy direction.
